EN = {
 'intro': "In episode 1.2, Alice started encrypting her orders with AES-256-CBC. Eve's guards still stop the messenger under her walls, but Eve only reads bytes that look drawn at random.\n\nShe has not given up: the scroll is in her hands. She cannot read it, but she can write on it.",
 'flip': "Today, Alice's order is “ATTACK AT 6AM TOMORROW”. Eve cannot read it, but she knows the shape of Alice's orders, from reading some in the clear: the hour is byte 11.\n\nAnd Bob decrypts the first block this way: P1 = AES⁻¹({3|C1}) ⊕ {2|IV}, where:\n- AES⁻¹ is the decryption of one block with the key;\n- ⊕ is XOR, which flips each bit where the other byte has a 1.\n\nSo each byte of the IV lands on the byte of the order in the same place. Eve XORs byte 11 of the {2|IV} with {r|0f}:\n- she flips its last 4 bits;\n- byte 11 of the order changes the same way: “6” (36 in hexadecimal) becomes “{r|9}” ({r|39}).",
 'ride': "Launch the messenger. Eve stops him, changes the byte of the IV, and lets him go on. Bob decrypts with the right key:\n- the padding is fine;\n- the text is readable;\n- nothing looks wrong.\n\nHe reads “ATTACK AT {r|9AM} TOMORROW”. Tomorrow at 6, Alice will attack alone, and Eve never needed the key.",
 'why': "Encryption hides the order, it does not protect it. AES-CBC decrypts any bytes at all and always gives something back: nothing in the ciphertext tells Bob whether it arrived as Alice wrote it.\n\nChanging a byte of the {3|ciphertext} works too:\n- the block she touched turns to noise;\n- the byte in the same place of the next block changes (the card shows it, on byte 1 of {3|C1}).\n\nWhat is missing is integrity: for Bob to see whether a single byte changed on the way.",
 'key': "Alice and Bob share a second secret key, also 32 bytes drawn at random, used only to seal: the {1|HMAC key}.\n\nWhy not the AES key? One key, one job: each key does a single thing, and a weakness in one job does not spread to the other.\n\nLike the AES key, it must reach Bob without passing Eve: that is episode 1.4. For now, they both have it, and Eve does not.",
 'hmac': "SHA-256 is a hash function. It takes as many bytes as you like and gives back a 32-byte fingerprint:\n- the same message always gives the same fingerprint;\n- a single changed byte gives an unrelated one.\n\nHMAC brings a key into the computation (two passes of SHA-256, the key in each): without the key, nobody can compute the right fingerprint of a message.\n\nAlice computes HMAC-SHA256 with the {1|HMAC key} over the {2|IV} and the {3|ciphertext} laid end to end (written {2|IV} ‖ {3|ciphertext}). These 32 bytes, the {4|seal}, travel with the packet.",
 'avalanche': "On arrival, Bob redoes the computation with his {1|HMAC key}, over the {2|IV} and the {3|ciphertext} he received, and compares it with the {4|seal} in the packet.\n\nIf Eve changed byte 11 of the IV, the seal Bob computes has nothing to do with Alice's: the card compares them, byte by byte, and the bytes that differ are {r|red}.\n\nCould Eve redo the seal of her changed packet? She would need the HMAC key.",
 'sealed': "Same order, same changed byte, but this time the packet carries its {4|seal}. Launch the messenger:\n- Eve changes the byte of the IV as before, with no way to redo the seal;\n- at his gate, Bob recomputes the seal and sees it does not match;\n- he rejects the packet without decrypting it.\n\nHe will not read “{r|9AM}”: he knows he has received no valid order, and waits for Alice to send one again.",
 'forged': "Eve tries something else: one of her riders, in Alice's colours, carries a packet she made from scratch. “ATTACK AT {r|MIDNIGHT}”, encrypted and sealed, but with keys of her own: she does not have Alice's.\n\nLaunch her rider. Bob recomputes the seal with his {1|HMAC key}, which is not Eve's, and rejects the packet.",
 'first': "Bob checks the seal first, and only decrypts packets whose seal is right.\n\nThe other way round, he would decrypt Eve's doctored packets, and his answers would tell her things:\n- a wrong padding gives an error;\n- a right one does not.\n\nSending Bob thousands of doctored packets, noting each time whether he complains about the padding, is enough to recover the order byte by byte, without the key: that is the **padding oracle** attack. A packet whose seal is wrong is rejected without ever being decrypted.",
 'compare': "One thing is left: the comparison itself. A Bob in a hurry would compare the two seals byte by byte and stop at the first one that differs. So he would refuse a seal wrong early a little faster than one wrong late:\n- wrong at byte 1, refused after a single comparison;\n- wrong at byte 10, refused after 10.\n\nBy timing his refusals, Eve would find the right seal of a changed packet, byte by byte. So Bob always compares all 32 bytes to the end, and answers only then: in Python, **hmac.compare_digest**.\n\nEpisode 8, the guard in a hurry, shows this attack in detail.",
 'packet': "What travels now:\n- {2|the IV}, 16 bytes;\n- {3|the ciphertext};\n- {4|the seal}, the HMAC of the IV and the ciphertext, 32 bytes.\n\nOne brick is missing: {1|both keys}, AES and HMAC, must reach Bob without Eve reading them, that is episode 1.4.\n\nAnd the seal proves only two things:\n- the packet comes from someone holding the HMAC key;\n- it has not changed.\n\nA real packet from Alice, kept by Eve and sent again the next day, would still pass: that is one of the tricks of episode 1.6.",
 'end': "In the sandbox, write your order, then choose:\n- what Eve does: nothing, a byte of the IV, a byte of the ciphertext, or her own rider;\n- whether Bob checks the seal.\n\nThe card shows what Bob decrypts, or why he refuses.\n\nEpisode 8, the guard in a hurry, shows the stopwatch attack on a hasty comparison. Episode 6, the penguin, shows a flipped bit in a CBC ciphertext up close.",
}
Q = r"""(?:'(?:[^'\\]|\\.)*'|"(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*")"""
src = F.read_text()
fr_at, en_at = src.index('steps: {'), src.index('steps: {', src.index('steps: {') + 1)
def put(src, start, end, d):
    for k, v in d.items():
        m = re.compile(r'(\b%s: \{ t: %s, p: )(%s)' % (k, Q, Q)).search(src, start, end)
        if not m: sys.exit(f'no step {k}')
        new = json.dumps(v, ensure_ascii=False)
        src = src[:m.start(2)] + new + src[m.end(2):]; end += len(new) - (m.end(2) - m.start(2))
        if start > m.start(2): pass
    return src, end
src, _ = put(src, en_at, len(src), EN)       # en first: it sits after fr, so fr's offsets hold
src, _ = put(src, fr_at, en_at, FR)
F.write_text(src)
